GRASP-GA Hybrid Based Supply Chain Order Assignment Optimization

Toy implementation of a real-world Inventory Routing Problem (IRP) based on the paper:

Perez, C., Climent, L., Nicolo, G., Arbelaez, A., & Salido, M. A.
A hybrid metaheuristic with learning for a real supply chain scheduling problem
Engineering Applications of Artificial Intelligence, 2023.

Overview

This project is a toy implementation of a real industrial supply chain scheduling problem.

The objective is to assign customer orders to feasible warehouses while minimizing:

Transportation cost
Extra production cost caused by inventory shortages

The implementation reproduces the core logic of the paper using a simplified GRASP-based approach.

Problem Description

In real supply chain operations:

Each order can only be assigned to a subset of feasible warehouses
Warehouses have different inventory levels
Transportation costs vary by warehouse
Inventory shortages generate additional production costs

Therefore, assigning orders only based on the nearest warehouse is not always optimal.

This project models the trade-off between:

Transportation efficiency
Inventory availability
Objective Function

Minimize:

Total Cost = Transportation Cost + Extra Production Cost

Constraints
Each order must be assigned to exactly one feasible warehouse
Inventory can become negative
Negative inventory means additional production is required
Additional production generates shortage cost
Implemented Logic
1. Constraint-Based Order Prioritization

Orders with:

fewer feasible warehouses
larger demand

are processed first.

This reflects the GRASP ordering logic from the original paper.

2. Local Search

For each order:

evaluate all feasible warehouses
compute:
transportation cost
inventory shortage cost
choose the warehouse with minimum weighted cost
3. Alpha Tuning

The algorithm dynamically adjusts the balance between:

transportation cost
shortage cost

depending on the current inventory situation.

4. Inventory Update

After assigning an order:

warehouse inventory is updated
future stock levels are adjusted

This simulates real operational inventory flow.

Technologies
Python
Greedy Randomized Adaptive Search Procedure (GRASP)
Example Output
O1 -> W1 | transport: 10 | shortage: 0
O2 -> W3 | transport: 15 | shortage: 20
...
Key Takeaways

Through this project, I learned:

Supply chain optimization requires balancing multiple operational costs
Inventory and transportation decisions should be integrated
Large-scale operational problems require both:
fast feasible solution generation
diversified search strategies
Future Improvements

Possible extensions:

Genetic Algorithm (GA) implementation
Meta-learning parameter tuning
Multi-echelon supply chain modeling
Vehicle routing integration
Stochastic demand simulation
Reference

Perez, C., Climent, L., Nicolo, G., Arbelaez, A., & Salido, M. A. (2023).
A hybrid metaheuristic with learning for a real supply chain scheduling problem.
Engineering Applications of Artificial Intelligence, 126, 107188.
